# where a locally-run process keeps its pidfile and log
.run/

# Compiled binaries.
#
# `go build ./services/...` from the workspace root writes each command's
# binary into the directory it was run from, or into the service directory,
# and a twenty megabyte file is easy to commit without noticing. Named by the
# convention every command here follows, so a new service is covered the day
# it is written: <name>d for a daemon, plus the two that predate it.
/core
/*d
services/*/core
services/*/dashboard
services/*/*d
# ...and what that pattern must not swallow, which turned out to be most of the
# repository.
#
# "*d" is far looser than it reads. Both "cmd" and "go.mod" end in d, so
# services/*/*d matched every service's cmd/ directory and every service's
# go.mod. Ten services' entrypoints and ten go.mod files were sitting untracked:
# `git add` on one said nothing, `git status` never listed it, and a clone of
# this repository would not build. It stayed invisible because a file already
# tracked ignores .gitignore, so the rule only ever bit a file that was new.
#
# The distinction that actually separates a compiled binary from source here is
# the extension: authd, gatewayd, core and dashboard have none, and every file
# worth committing has one. So re-include anything with a dot, plus the cmd
# directories themselves — a pattern cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, which is why cmd/ is negated as a directory rather than
# by its contents.
!services/*/cmd/
!services/*/*.*
# frontend
node_modules/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
